{"name":"Favourite Jeans","hex":"#8aa3b1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"favourite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Most-liked. (In other words, [person]'s favorite [noun] = the [noun] that [person] likes the most.)","Liked, cherished."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person or thing who enjoys special regard or favour.","A person who is preferred or trusted above all others."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/favourite"},{"term":"jeans","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pair of trousers made from denim cotton."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of jean"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jeans"}],"entries":[{"title":"Favourite","blocks":[{"text":"In history and politics, a favourite was the intimate companion of a ruler or other important person. In post-classical and early-modern Europe, among other times and places, the term was used of individuals delegated significant political power by a ruler. It was especially a phenomenon of the 16th and 17th centuries, when government had become too complex for many hereditary rulers with no great interest in or talent for it, and political institutions were still evolving. From 1600 to 1660 there were particular successions of all-powerful minister-favourites in much of Europe, particularly in Spain, England, France and Sweden."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg/330px-Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Favourite"},{"title":"Jeans","blocks":[{"text":"Jeans are a type of trousers made from denim or dungaree cloth. Often the term \"jeans\" refers to a particular style of trousers, called \"blue jeans\", with the addition of copper pocket rivets added by Jacob W. Davis in 1871 and patented by Davis and Levi Strauss on May 20, 1873. Prior to the patent, the term \"blue jeans\" had been long in use for various items of workwear, constructed from a heavy blue-colored denim fabric."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Jeans.jpg/330px-Jeans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeans"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg/1280px-Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Duke of Buckingham by the workshop of Rubens (1617–1628)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APortrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_(by_Peter_Paul_Rubens).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Triple_Portrait_of_Cardinal_de_Richelieu_probably_1642%2C_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg/500px-Triple_Portrait_of_Cardinal_de_Richelieu_probably_1642%2C_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Triple_Portrait_of_Cardinal_de_Richelieu_probably_1642%2C_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg/1280px-Triple_Portrait_of_Cardinal_de_Richelieu_probably_1642%2C_Philippe_de_C